#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/id #uuid "9552ad30-9d46-5884-9877-18ca8842c252" :question/math-academy-id "e-4775" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf $\\langle x_1,x_2 \\rangle$ and $\\langle y_1,y_2 \\rangle$ are the solutions to the systems $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $L\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, what is the value of $x_1 \\cdot y_2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}$. Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis gives the system of equations\n${y_{1} = 6; - 2y_{1} + y_{2} =-8$.\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4$. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}$. Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis gives the system of equations\n${7x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 6; - 2x_{2} = 4$.\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2$. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "24bc4954-462b-5066-83d4-8a4a87efc7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4777" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$, find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n${Ly = b; Ux = y$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nWe now solve\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = 2; y_{1} + y_{2} = 4; y_{1} - 2y_{2} + y_{3} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= 2, \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4 - y_{1} \\\\\n&= 4 - (2) \\\\\n&= 2, \\\\\ny_{3} &= 1 - y_{1} + 2y_{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - (2) + 2(2) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5250e0e1-4909-5075-8a8e-aa2f1be0bd9a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cbefba9a-eda2-5a8e-8bb5-59ea4175b835" :question/math-academy-id "e-8926" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n${Ly = b; Ux = y$\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nWe now solve\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = 5; 15y_{1} + y_{2} = 66\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8e8eeb2-e460-525b-b83b-0cefde5b0d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116596} {:answer/id #uuid "e5632857-fcc6-5529-9013-36dcdc488696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116597} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b956a9-3e83-59e1-a072-df57460b7c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116598} {:answer/id #uuid "37018c59-4769-58b3-8e67-fc888628b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116599} {:answer/id #uuid "47d1a930-1a53-5630-a9ae-8ba4ca728ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b956a9-3e83-59e1-a072-df57460b7c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116598} :answer-field/id #uuid "9daf47c2-7d9f-58bf-8651-26039aeaed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116595}] :question/id #uuid "cc6bf17a-cfc4-5f52-984e-78af80897af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105949" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U), b = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $⟨x_{1}, x_{2}⟩$ and $⟨y_{1}, y_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax = b$ and $Ly = b$ respectively, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot y_{1}$?"}]}
